+# Ajv vs Zod: validating draft 2020-12 JSON Schema in Node
+
+Question: see `QUESTION.md`. Date: 2026-11-11.
+
+**Evidence status: none of this has been verified in this run.** The repo has no
+`node_modules` (checked: the only tracked file is `QUESTION.md`), and permission to fetch
+web pages was not granted. Every claim below is **inferred from prior knowledge** of the
+official docs, not read during this session. Check the cited pages before relying on it.
+
+## Answer
+
+**Use Ajv.** Ajv is a JSON Schema validator and supports draft 2020-12 through a separate
+class. Zod is not a JSON Schema validator. It defines schemas in TypeScript and can *export*
+them as JSON Schema, but it does not take an arbitrary 2020-12 schema document and validate
+data against it to spec.
+
+## Findings
+
+| Claim | Source (not fetched) | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| Ajv supports drafts 04, 06, 07, 2019-09 and 2020-12 | https://ajv.js.org/json-schema.html (Ajv v8) | inferred |
+| You enable 2020-12 with `Ajv2020` from `ajv/dist/2020` (`import Ajv2020 from "ajv/dist/2020"`). The default `Ajv` export targets draft-07 | https://ajv.js.org/json-schema.html#draft-2020-12, https://ajv.js.org/guide/schema-language.html | inferred |
+| Draft 2020-12 and draft-07 schemas cannot be mixed in one Ajv instance | https://ajv.js.org/json-schema.html | inferred |
+| Zod defines schemas in TypeScript with a builder API (`z.object(...)`) and parses data against them. It does not take JSON Schema documents as input | https://zod.dev | inferred |
+| Zod 4's `z.toJSONSchema()` *outputs* JSON Schema, with draft-2020-12 as the default target (`target` option also allows draft-7) | https://zod.dev/json-schema | inferred |
